# Curia — Licence

## Free for everyone during the Lex beta

Curia depends on the UK Government's **i.AI Lex** service, which is currently a
**beta / experimental** service. While that remains the case, **Curia is free
for everyone to use — including commercial and for-profit organisations such as
law firms.**

**Free Use Grant (beta period).** In addition to the PolyForm Noncommercial
licence below, the licensor grants every user — regardless of whether their use
is commercial — a worldwide, royalty-free licence to use, copy, modify and
distribute Curia on the same terms as the PolyForm Noncommercial License 1.0.0,
as if their purpose were a permitted noncommercial purpose. This extra grant is
temporary: the licensor may withdraw it **for future versions** of Curia by a
notice in the project repository giving at least 30 days' warning. Withdrawal
does not affect copies already obtained under this grant. Commercial licensing
terms may be introduced for later versions; see [`LICENSING.md`](LICENSING.md).

## Underlying base licence

When the Free Use Grant above no longer applies to a given version, that version
is licensed as follows:

- **Non-profit / noncommercial use** — free under the **PolyForm Noncommercial
  License 1.0.0** (full text below). This covers individuals, charities, law
  clinics, advice organisations, educational and public research institutions,
  and government bodies. No fee, no sign-up.
- **Commercial use** (e.g. fee-charging law firms and other for-profit
  organisations) — would require a separate commercial licence, if and when the
  licensor chooses to offer one. See [`LICENSING.md`](LICENSING.md).

This licence covers **Curia's own source code only**. It does not change the
licences of the software Curia builds on (e.g. i.AI **Lex**, which is MIT) or
the terms of the underlying data sources (legislation.gov.uk / GOV.UK under the
Open Government Licence, Find Case Law under the Open Justice Licence, etc.).
See [`NOTICE.md`](NOTICE.md) for third-party software and data attributions.

Required Notice: Copyright (c) 2026 The Curia maintainers

